{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-16686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the points where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=24$ intercepts the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-16686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-24 & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot {0}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-24 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-24 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =6 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,-\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,\\sqrt{6}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-16686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f0616c027bd3fc737991598e69577236f22ca130980f54841042acf71eb8364b"} {"question" "q-16686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-16686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,\\pm 3)"] ["math" "(0,\\pm 6)"] ["math" "(0,\\pm \\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(0,\\pm \\sqrt{6})"] ["text" "There is no $y$-intercept"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-16686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,\\pm \\sqrt{6})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-72913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-20x+6y+47=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-72913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-20x+6y+47 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3(0)^{2}-20x+6(0)+47 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-20x+47 & =0 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-10x]+47 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x-5)^{2}-25]+47 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-50+47 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ x & =5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(5-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)$ and $(5+\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-72913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f0616c027bd3fc737991598e69577236f22ca130980f54841042acf71eb8364b"} {"question" "q-72913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-72913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(10\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)"] ["math" "(10\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)"] ["math" "(5\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)"] ["math" "(5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)"] ["text" "There is no $x$-intercept"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-72913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-327143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the point where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-12x+8y+4=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-327143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-12x+8y+4 & =0 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+4{y}^{2}-12(0)+8y+4 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+8y+4 & =0 \\\\ 4\\,[{y}^{2}+2y]+4 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y+1)^{2}-1]+4 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+1)^{2}-4+4 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+1 & =0 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/assets/efaa5f474c5f505f6a1ca98469a77f8d21e059adee99c60cb9a2a7cb87600b5f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-327143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f0616c027bd3fc737991598e69577236f22ca130980f54841042acf71eb8364b"} {"question" "q-327143" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-327143" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-327143" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-327143" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-327144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the point where the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}-40x+100y+100=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-327144" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}-40x+100y+100 & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+25{y}^{2}-40(0)+100y+100 & =0 \\\\ 25{y}^{2}+100y+100 & =0 \\\\ 25\\,[{y}^{2}+4y+4] & =0 \\\\ 25\\,(y+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/assets/78be4ca7215ee8dbf05a442080bae752e4fadfbe76084d975338d00e1bb02c78.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/state.json" "file_sha256" "df582b5d39f369aa387af1def6a4d18679d739a99c6f2e2e4589e4b7a026ec82"} {"question" "q-327144" "field" nil "attribute" "questi